course: dictionary
lesson: 11041
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, look at the words around it. This is context at work. «Owls, foxes, cats, weasels, snakes and hawks all hunt mice» sits in a list of hunters, so here a weasel must be an animal.
explain: The clues can also show a feeling. «Ever since, the weasels have been living in Toad Hall, lying in bed half the day, eating Toad's food» tells you the weasels are sly villains who have taken over someone else's home. Nobody is being kind here.
explain: Ask yourself: which meaning fits the sentence? Then try it. If the animal meaning does not make sense, try the sneaky-person meaning, or the other meanings the dictionary gives.
fact: A weasel is a small, thin, fast wild animal with brown fur and a long body.
fact: Weasels hunt mice and voles.
fact: Weasel can also mean a sneaky person who cannot be trusted. This meaning is informal and rude.
fact: To weasel out of something means to avoid doing it in a sneaky way.
fact: Weasel words are vague words used to avoid saying something clearly.
exercise: «Owls, foxes, cats, weasels, snakes and hawks all hunt mice.» In this sentence, what is a weasel? | A mouse's home | *A small, thin, fast wild animal | A sneaky person | A kind of cheese
exercise: Tom says about Dan, «He's such a weasel, he'll never own up.» What does weasel mean here? | *A sneaky person you cannot trust | A small brown animal | A fast runner | A friendly neighbour
exercise: Ben tried to weasel out of tidying his room. What does weasel out of mean? | To finish it early | To do something very fast | *To avoid doing it in a sneaky way | To ask a grown-up for help
test: In English lessons, what are weasel words? | Words about small animals | Words from a famous book | Long words that are hard to spell | *Vague words used to avoid saying something clearly
test: In «The weasels would be feasting in the warm while they were cut to pieces in the cold», what kind of weasels are they? | *Sly enemies who are enjoying themselves at others' expense | Kind neighbours | Hungry mice | Brave soldiers
exam: Which meaning fits weasel in «Owls, foxes, cats, weasels, snakes and hawks all hunt mice»? | Vague words used in adverts | A sneaky person | *A small wild animal that hunts mice | To avoid doing something
